  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through next Saturday) \nIssued at 1130 PM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nHot and dry conditions will continue Sunday and into next week,\nwith upper-level ridging remaining in place across the region\nthrough next next weekend. Daily high and low temperatures will\nsee little change over the next 7 days, with afternoon highs\nreaching into the mid 90s and up to around 104 degrees, and \novernight lows in the 70s. The warmest temperatures are expected \nacross the Big Country and northern portions of the Concho Valley \nand Heartland, with the hottest day next week being Wednesday.\nSouth winds will continue each day, but expect wind speeds should\nto be a little lower Sunday through Tuesday as the surface \npressure gradient weakens to some extent. Increased south winds \nare indicated Wednesday.\n\nSome models are beginning to show potential for an upper-level\ndisturbance and associated surface \"cold\" front tracking into West\nCentral Texas from the northwest overnight Wednesday into \nThursday morning. Models like the 18Z GFS were most aggressive \nwith showing this system tracking through the area and a closed \nupper-level low lingering over the area through next weekend. If \nthis were to pan out, West Central texas could see a slight \nreprieve from the heat and chances for precipitation. However, \nthis remains an outlier in the model spread, and at this time \nthere is very low confidence in this scenario coming to fruition. \nAt this time, the more probable solution is that the upper-level \ntrough and associated low stay further north, and we'll likely \njust see a slight uptick in cloud coverage and continued hot and \ndry temperatures through next weekend.",
